# Line endings are CONTENT in this tree, not formatting. Two rule blocks,
# opposite directions, each the fix for a measured defect. They are ordered
# fixtures-first for a reason the second block states.
#
# ADOPTERS: copy this file. Without it the kit's scripts do not run on a Windows
# checkout, and the fixtures whose Windows endings ARE the assertion quietly
# stop asserting. It claims nothing about your own files -- see WHAT IT DOES NOT
# TOUCH, below.
#
#
# WHY THE EXECUTABLES ARE PINNED: WITHOUT IT THE KIT DOES NOT RUN AT ALL
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is git's DEFAULT ON WINDOWS, and it converts every text
# file on checkout -- including the shell scripts. A .sh file with carriage
# returns is not a shell script any more; the interpreter reads the return as
# part of the last word on the line:
#
#     $ sh docs/adr/adr-lint.sh
#     docs/adr/adr-lint.sh: line 27: : command not found
#     docs/adr/adr-lint.sh: line 28: set: -: invalid option
#
# Measured on a real clone: TWELVE of twelve executables the kit ships would not
# run. Six linters (adr, prd, link, agents, audit-record, pr-link), the
# discipline-test runner, the two checks run by hand (expect-check,
# provenance-check), and all three git hooks -- including the pre-commit hook
# that is supposed to enforce the gate. The whole quality gate, silent, on the
# platform where the kit is most likely to be adopted cold.
#
# It is also why the CRLF fixtures could not have been exercised by the adopter
# they were written for: that adopter never reached the linter.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises the blob to line feeds AND forces line feeds in the
# working tree, whatever `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# WHAT THIS BUYS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It makes the scripts RUNNABLE, nothing
# more. Every ordinary Markdown file still arrives with carriage returns, and
# reading those correctly is each linter's own job. Measured on a clone across
# the six linters plus expect-check.sh: 7 of 7 green, against 0 of 7 before the
# work that added this rule -- nothing ran at all -- and 3 of 7 with this rule
# alone and two of the six linters fixed. So the halves are separable and both
# are needed: delete this rule and nothing runs; keep it and revert the linters
# and four of the seven fail on a tree they should accept.
#
# (Those seven are not the seven commands AGENTS.md lists under "Checks you can
# run", which are the same six linters plus `git diff --check`.)
#
#
# WHY FIVE FIXTURE CASES ARE PINNED THE OTHER WAY
#
# One per linter that reads Markdown -- adr, links, agents, tasks -- plus
# docs/links/tests/bad-crlf-expect/, which is CRLF down to its EXPECT file so
# that expect-check.sh's own strip has a case. Their files carry Windows line
# endings, and they are tests only while they keep them.
#
# The risk is a COMMIT, not a checkout. `core.autocrlf=input` converts CRLF to
# LF when a file is STAGED and does nothing on checkout; `true` converts in both
# directions. So the way these fixtures lose their carriage returns is someone
# whose git is set to `input` or `true` re-committing them -- the returns are
# stripped on the way into the blob and never come back. Measured: with a CRLF
# blob, a clone under `input` still delivers CRLF, and it is the committer's
# setting that decides.
#
# Every one of them would then pass while testing nothing: a green with no
# assertion behind it, which is the class of defect the fixtures exist to close.
# run-discipline-tests.sh guards against that TWICE, and reads these rules
# rather than holding a copy of them.
#
# `eol=crlf` keeps the blob as line feeds -- so the diff stays readable and the
# files stay mergeable -- and forces carriage returns in the WORKING TREE on
# every platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to.
#
# Scoped to those five case directories on purpose. A repository-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would HIDE the carriage-return defect rather than fix it
# (a workaround under R4), and would impose a line-ending policy on every
# adopter's checkout, which is not this kit's business.
#
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH, AND THE LIMIT THAT LEAVES
#
# Nothing outside the paths named below. Your own scripts are caught only if you
# name one `docs/<area>/<x>-lint.sh` or put one under `docs/<area>/tests/`; a
# `docs/scripts/build.sh`, a `scripts/deploy.sh` or a vendored tree resolves
# `unspecified`. That is a claim, so it is stated rather than left to be found.
#
# The cost of that narrowness: an executable added or moved OUTSIDE these paths
# loses the pin silently -- `install.sh`, `bin/armature` and
# `scripts/bootstrap.sh` all resolve `unspecified` today. Unlike the fixtures,
# no mechanism here is adopter-safe: a check enumerating every .sh in the
# repository would fire on the adopter's own, which is the problem the narrow
# form exists to solve. Recorded rather than half-mechanised.
#
#
# --- the fixture cases whose carriage returns ARE the test --------------------
#
# If you ever need to pin a path holding a SPACE -- this tree ships two, under
# docs/adr/tests/ and docs/prd/tests/ -- note that gitattributes cannot express
# one. A literal space ends the pattern, so `good-path with space/**` is read as
# the pattern `good-path` plus the attribute names `with` and `space/**`, and
# git reports `is not a valid attribute name` against a token that looks
# unrelated while the path itself silently resolves `unspecified`. Backslash
# escaping and a bracket class both fail the same way. Only the `?` wildcard
# works: `good-path?with?space/**`. Neither spaced fixture needs pinning today
# -- their endings are not the assertion -- so nothing here does this; it is
# written down because the obvious attempt fails quietly.
docs/adr/tests/good-crlf/**    eol=crlf
docs/links/tests/good-crlf/**  eol=crlf
docs/agents/tests/good-crlf/** eol=crlf
docs/tasks/tests/good-crlf/**  eol=crlf
# A bad case that is CRLF throughout, EXPECT file included, so expect-check.sh's
# own carriage-return strip has a case. It reads EXPECT and demands that id in
# the linter's output; without the strip the id is `L1` plus a return, which
# matches nothing, and every case in that suite fails while the linter beside it
# is right.
#
# The case is NAMED crlf on purpose: that is what puts it inside the runner's
# case-name check, so deleting the line below cannot be silent. Pinning a single
# file inside a case named anything else would sit outside that check.
docs/links/tests/bad-crlf-expect/** eol=crlf

# --- executable text: line feeds, or it will not run -------------------------
#
# THIS BLOCK COMES SECOND ON PURPOSE. Git resolves each attribute by LAST MATCH,
# per attribute. With the executable rules first, a helper script added inside
# one of the crlf fixtures above would take `text` from here and `eol=crlf` from
# there, and be unrunnable on EVERY platform rather than only on Windows. No
# such file exists; the ordering means one never can.
#
# PINNED BY THE KIT'S OWN NAMING, not by directory. Two wider forms were tried
# and both reached too far. `*.sh` carries no slash, so git matches it against
# the BASENAME AT EVERY DEPTH -- it governed every .sh file in an adopter's
# repository, vendored trees included. `docs/**/*.sh` looked anchored and was
# not: `docs/` is one of the commonest directory names anywhere, and this kit is
# designed to land in one that already exists, so an adopter's own
# `docs/scripts/build.sh` committed with carriage returns resolved `eol=lf` on
# contact and lost them at their next commit -- silently, in a file nobody
# opened.
#
# The forms below match what the kit actually ships: `<area>-lint.sh` linters,
# the runner by name, and any script under a fixture `tests/` directory.
#
# `.githooks/*` and `.githooks/**/*.sh` rather than `.githooks/**`: the wide form
# set an explicit `text` on everything under that directory, which switches OFF
# git's binary auto-detection there, so a binary helper dropped into
# .githooks/tests/ later would be converted rather than skipped. These two forms
# reach every executable the kit ships -- the three hooks, and provenance-check.sh
# one level down -- without claiming anything about a file that is not one.
docs/*/*-lint.sh                   text eol=lf
docs/tests/run-discipline-tests.sh text eol=lf
docs/*/tests/**/*.sh               text eol=lf
.githooks/*                        text eol=lf
.githooks/**/*.sh                  text eol=lf
